00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.700
Welcome to Horror History.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.500
The Final Destination franchise has always
done a great deal to pay tribute to the horror

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.530
genre, naming many characters after classic
horror icons.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.610
You can learn all about that in my Things
You Missed episodes, but with William Bludworth,

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:20.040
they paid a different kind of tribute by casting
The Candyman himself, Tony Todd.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.200
So don’t be surprised if you see his face
on another episode of Horror History one day.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.440
Before I get into his history, there is an
important note I have to bring up.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.520
It seems there are actually multiple universes
within Final Destination.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.710
In the Final Destination 1 universe, the Volée
Flight 180 crash takes place on May 13th,

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.550
1999, but in the sequel universe, it takes
place on May 13th, 2000.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:43.410
It’s certainly possible that this was just
a mistake, but I prefer to cover it up with

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.730
the existence of two timelines.

00:01:47.241 --> 00:01:49.038
“You think they’re still up there?

00:01:49.038 --> 00:01:49.932
Somehow.

00:01:49.932 --> 00:01:51.638
Flight 180 are they still on the flight?

00:01:51.638 --> 00:01:52.556
Somewhere.

00:01:52.556 --> 00:01:53.556
Are they safe?”

00:01:53.556 --> 00:01:57.215
“I have thought a lot about that somewhere
Alex.

00:01:57.215 --> 00:01:58.695
It exists, that place.”

00:01:58.695 --> 00:02:02.340
So for the purposes of this video, I’ll
be analysing the story based on the events

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.120
of the sequel timeline, because it contains
the most information on Bludworth’s story.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:10.000
To understand who Mr. Bludworth really is,
let’s take it back to his earliest chronological

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.528
appearance in the series.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.920
William Bludworth’s earliest known appearance
was as the funeral held by Presage Paper for

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.310
the 17 employees who perished during the North
Bay Bridge Collapse of 2000.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:29.401
He tried to warn two of the survivors, Sam
and Peter, what’s about to happen to them.

00:02:29.401 --> 00:02:35.926
“Death doesn’t like to be cheated.”

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.420
They chalk it up to him just being a creepy
guy, and he goes on his way, whistling.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.970
Right from the beginning, Bludworth knows
about how Death goes after those who escaped

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.390
it before, and Death would reclaim one of
the victims not long after.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:51.050
Bludworth is called to Westdale College to
pick up the remains of Candice Hooper, who

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:53.990
had suffered a horrible accident in her gymnastics
routine.

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:56.980
This accident, it wasn’t just like the judges
gave her a six.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:57.779
Like she died.

00:02:57.779 --> 00:02:59.006
She literally died.

00:03:01.638 --> 00:03:03.505
*screams*

00:03:03.753 --> 00:03:06.253
“What’s up guys... I never wanted to make a video like this.”

00:03:06.253 --> 00:03:10.180
At the scene of the accident, Bludworth makes
eye contact with Sam, the man whose vision

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.959
saved himself and seven others during the
bridge collapse.

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:16.620
The next victim that death catches up with
is Isaac Palmer, who was unable to overcome

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.340
a series of accidents that occured during
his massage.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:23.140
Bludworth is once again tasked with picking
up the body, but this time, he’s recognized

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:26.140
by Sam, who asks him why he’s at the scene
of each accident.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:29.239
William tells him that he’s simply doing
his job, and explains that he warned them

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:33.950
at the funeral because he’s seen what happens
to accident survivors before.

00:03:34.836 --> 00:03:43.269
“A lucky few survive a disaster, and then
one by one, Death comes for them all.”

00:03:43.269 --> 00:03:47.060
He also tells them that they “changed things”
on the bridge, that they created what he calls

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.550
a wrinkle in reality.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:52.750
This means he’s not only aware of how Death
operates, but he knows that something caused

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.690
one of them to alter the course of history
in order to escape.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:59.770
He does offer a solution though, if they let
Death take someone else in their place, they

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.239
may be able to take that person’s remaining
lifespan.

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:04.450
“Then the books are balanced.”

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.970
William presumably is also responsible for
cleaning up after Olivia, Dennis and Nathan,

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:12.250
after each of them is unable to avoid Death
during a two week span at the beginning of

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:13.250
May 2000.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:15.550
Remember, we’re using the sequel timeline.

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:19.709
He hears about how six students and a teacher
got off of Volée Flight 180 before it’s

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:23.259
crash on May 13th, and expects that two of
the survivors will be visiting him at the

00:04:23.259 --> 00:04:24.409
morgue before long.

00:04:24.409 --> 00:04:28.229
This tells us that he’s not just an expert
on death, he seems to be able to predict when

00:04:28.229 --> 00:04:31.447
things are going to happen and even knows
people’s names when they’ve never met

00:04:31.447 --> 00:04:32.058
before.

00:04:32.058 --> 00:04:36.020
His first job after the plane crash was to
pick up and examine Tod Waggner, who was previously

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:40.830
believed to be a suicide, until William discovered
cuticle lacerations; proving Tod had actually

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:44.721
been pulling on the wire that wrapped around
his neck and choked him -- he was trying to

00:04:44.721 --> 00:04:45.485
escape.

00:04:45.485 --> 00:04:49.710
As predicted, he does get a pair of visitors
that night, Alex Browning and Clear Rivers.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.809
He tries to get his point across to them.

00:04:51.809 --> 00:04:55.550
“In death there are no accidents.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:56.789
No coincidences.

00:04:56.789 --> 00:04:58.039
No mishaps.

00:04:58.039 --> 00:04:59.909
And no escapes.”

00:04:59.909 --> 00:05:04.089
He tells them everything that happens is part
of Death’s design, that their friend’s

00:05:04.089 --> 00:05:06.169
accident proves it now has a new design for
them.

00:05:06.169 --> 00:05:09.939
They must figure out when their next appointment
with Death is before it arrives.

00:05:09.939 --> 00:05:14.279
Bludworth’s seemingly all-knowing apprehension
about Death, his generally creepy demeanor,

00:05:14.279 --> 00:05:17.500
his voice, and just the fact that his name
is William Bludworth like he’s some kind

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:22.110
of 2000s alt-metal frontman have led many
fans to believe that he is the human incarnation

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.600
of Death, but I think this scene says otherwise.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.890
For one, if Death is trying to reclaim the
lives of those who escaped it’s grasp, why

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:32.720
would he be warning them by giving them a
heads up that Death is coming back at them.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.869
If Bludworth was Death, he probably wouldn’t
want them to know he’s coming for them.

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:39.879
He also refers to Death and The Grim Reaper
in the third person.

00:05:39.879 --> 00:05:43.580
“And you don’t even want to **** with
that mack daddy!”

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:48.199
So, while I don’t believe that he’s the
human embodiment of Death himself, I do have

00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:52.009
a theory about who he really is, which I’ll
get into later on in his story.

00:05:52.009 --> 00:05:55.759
This time, he doesn’t tell about the loophole
where you can theoretically take someone else’s

00:05:55.759 --> 00:05:57.550
life and be awarded their remaining years.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:01.189
I like to think this is because he actually
has good intentions, and doesn’t want to

00:06:01.189 --> 00:06:04.899
see one of them turn into a killer like Peter
Friedkin did a couple weeks before.

00:06:04.899 --> 00:06:06.629
So basically, he learned his lesson.

00:06:06.629 --> 00:06:10.940
He says goodbye to Clear and Alex, telling
Alex he’ll be seeing him soon, another example

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:13.659
of Bludworth seeming to know what’s in store…

00:06:13.659 --> 00:06:17.360
We don’t see him for a while after that,
but we can assume he’s involved in the autopsy

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.839
of Terry Chaney, Valerie Lewton, Billy Hitchcock,
and eventually Alex Browning.

00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:26.439
Exactly one year after Volée Flight 180,
there is another major accident, and once

00:06:26.439 --> 00:06:32.800
again, William Bludworth finds himself tangled
up with the survivors.

00:06:39.819 --> 00:06:43.740
The morning before the next accident, a girl
named Kimberly Corman hears Bludworth’s

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.909
voice call out her name as she wakes up, but
does not yet know whose voice it is.

00:06:47.909 --> 00:06:52.439
What follows is a huge pile-up on Route 23
in Mt. Abraham, New York that leaves William

00:06:52.439 --> 00:06:56.719
with a lot of work to do -- there are 18 casualties,
and there would be many more to come, due

00:06:56.719 --> 00:07:00.699
to the fact that Kimberly prevented herself
and seven other potential victims from getting

00:07:00.699 --> 00:07:02.879
on the road after having a premonition.

00:07:02.879 --> 00:07:07.069
Like the survivors of previous accidents,
they too start to perish, only this time the

00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:10.330
order of their accidents is reversed from
the order they were destined to die in during

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:11.330
the pile-up.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.619
The first to go after the pile-up is Evan
Lewis, and while William takes care of the

00:07:14.619 --> 00:07:19.069
body, Clear, the lone remaining survivor from
Flight 180 takes Kimberly and another Route

00:07:19.069 --> 00:07:23.439
23 escapee back to his morgue to try to get
some more information on how to survive.

00:07:23.439 --> 00:07:26.229
William is expecting them when they arrive.

00:07:27.315 --> 00:07:28.959
“Hello Clear.

00:07:32.489 --> 00:07:34.429
I’ve been expecting you.”

00:07:34.429 --> 00:07:37.189
This time he tells them that only new life
can defeat Death.

00:07:37.189 --> 00:07:42.810
“The introduction of life that was not meant
to be… that can invalidate the list.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.439
Force Death to start anew.”

00:07:45.439 --> 00:07:49.121
He also calls Kimberly by her name without
her ever telling him, and that’s the end

00:07:49.121 --> 00:07:50.330
of the interaction.

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:54.909
So if not Death, who is William Bludworth,
and how do I explain his uncanny knowledge

00:07:54.909 --> 00:07:56.449
and ability to see the future?

00:07:56.449 --> 00:08:00.669
Well, in each Final Destination movie, the
protagonists have one thing in common, they

00:08:00.669 --> 00:08:04.449
are the visionaries that have premonitions
to help them avoid Death’s design.

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:08.300
My theory is that long ago, William Bludworth
was one of these visionaries.

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:11.110
He realized Death was coming for him, and
he avoided it.

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:14.989
However, he was much more gifted than Alex,
Kimberly, Wendy, Nick or Sam.

00:08:14.989 --> 00:08:19.360
And he was able to avoid Death not one time,
but continually, and as time went on, he got

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:21.479
better and better at seeing the signs.

00:08:21.479 --> 00:08:25.289
As he became more in tune with his clairvoyant
abilities, he started to learn how to control

00:08:25.289 --> 00:08:28.969
them, and was eventually able to look into
the future more or less at will, not just

00:08:28.969 --> 00:08:30.815
when there was a major accident ahead.

00:08:34.763 --> 00:08:38.870
He ended up becoming a coroner so that he
could study Death, and find the patterns that

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:42.800
Death used to take out others to ensure he
does not make the same mistakes.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:47.279
That’s why he seems to already have an understanding
of what Alex went through before walking off

00:08:47.279 --> 00:08:48.750
of the plane when they first meet.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:52.600
I think it’s even possible that he has a
bit of a psychic ability as well, and he may

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:55.889
have been the one to cause Kimberly to have
her vision, thus explaining why she heard

00:08:55.889 --> 00:08:58.002
his voice as she awoke the morning of.

00:08:58.002 --> 00:08:59.835
“Kimberly.”

00:08:59.835 --> 00:09:03.630
When he detects these accidents, it’s in
his best interest for survivors to escape,

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:07.129
because then he’ll get to see how Death
comes back for them and learn even more about

00:09:07.129 --> 00:09:08.129
Death’s tendencies.

00:09:08.129 --> 00:09:12.190
This could maybe even explain why Death eventually
tries to clean up the evidence after taking

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:16.610
out Tod, by having the water that he slipped
on recede, making it look like a suicide,

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:19.130
but Bludworth, to his credit, doesn’t fall
for it.

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:23.290
So he’s actually kind of a good guy, even
if he only really has his own best interests

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:24.290
in mind.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:26.250
Finally, this could also explain his disappearance.

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:29.752
He does first make an appearance in the novel,
Final Destination: Death of the Senses, where

00:09:29.752 --> 00:09:33.540
he fills in for a coroner in New York City
where he’s visited by serial killer escapees

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:37.519
Jack Curtis and Amy Tom, and gives them the
usual vague advice.

00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:41.269
He doesn’t appear again after that, but
we do hear his voice in Final Destination

00:09:41.269 --> 00:09:45.999
3, seemingly warning Wendy Christensen about
the danger that awaits her on Devil’s Flight.

00:09:46.861 --> 00:09:49.903
“HAHAHAHA!

00:09:49.903 --> 00:09:53.031
You can run!

00:09:53.031 --> 00:09:56.790
But you cannot hide…

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:59.847
This is the beginning of the end.”

00:09:59.847 --> 00:10:01.776
🎵 This is the beginning! 🎵

00:10:01.949 --> 00:10:05.629
After that, he doesn’t show up again in
the original 5 Final Destination films.

00:10:05.629 --> 00:10:09.450
If he really is Death’s longest standing
target, it was only a matter of time before

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:11.439
The Grim Reaper caught up with him.

00:10:11.439 --> 00:10:16.019
Afterall, he knows better than anyone that
Death does not like to be cheated.

00:10:16.019 --> 00:10:19.310
If you want to learn about the histories of
other Final Destination characters, click

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:21.000
that playlist on the left.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:25.251
And if you love horror, remember to subscribe
to CZsWorld for new horrors every week, ring

00:10:25.251 --> 00:10:29.709
the deathbell for notifications and “I’ll
see you soon.”

00:10:29.709 --> 00:10:31.540
Assuming we both survive.

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:34.959
If you want to beat death, use a facemask
and social distancing.

